#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257281 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "13e08fd0-4f41-56eb-8650-b72aa739b7a1" :knowledge-point/title "Initial Value Problems and Initial Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39ee5fc1-03e0-57e5-bbbf-315d46f4703b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257283} {:answer/id #uuid "f57d7578-82c4-5967-a523-96974dbf056c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257284} {:answer/id #uuid "9d8e1b40-531d-5738-bd3c-ddd411adcbb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257285} {:answer/id #uuid "3dac5233-1cb8-538b-a95f-9c3184be17d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257286} {:answer/id #uuid "c9953a88-e46e-55ac-b3c0-ab066edb021f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257287} {:answer/id #uuid "b345a296-774e-57b4-8d0e-503947db627b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257288} {:answer/id #uuid "9d54d347-85b6-56fb-b78d-46ec06bb4b9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257289} {:answer/id #uuid "d2471a44-b1ef-5141-9fe5-a87c669720c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257290}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3dac5233-1cb8-538b-a95f-9c3184be17d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257286} :answer-field/id #uuid "39594d48-3439-593f-beb0-784efbc73181" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257282}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7d3e1743-cd34-559d-bad0-d34093686fd6" :question/math-academy-id "q-118786" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\nI. $(1+{x}^{2}){y}^{'}=8y,\\,y(-1)=3$\nII. $8{y}^{'}-xy=3$\nIII. $3{y}^{''}=4y,\\,y(-1)=0,\\,{y}^{'}(-1)=1$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{'}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $(1+{x}^{2}){y}^{'}=8y$ together with the initial condition $y(-1)=3.$\n\n- System II is not a first-order initial value problem. We have a first-order ordinary differential equation, but there is no initial condition.\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $3{y}^{''}=4y$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(-1)=0,\\,{y}^{'}(-1)=1.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294238 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff04ceeb-c9f6-5562-b494-204eb62d7f5b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of the Points of Intersection of a Trigonometric Curve and a Horizontal Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c3c3b7fa-7ec4-58a0-a5f6-2c752977ed30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294240} {:answer/id #uuid "8d621446-a4c1-5070-b626-77ed872c6c20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294241} {:answer/id #uuid "df24ba6b-c0c0-53dc-ac84-f8a75abf42dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294242} {:answer/id #uuid "f5420b55-8b2c-5d50-8551-0c8f85248e33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294243} {:answer/id #uuid "cbbeaf68-b5dd-5406-a2f6-7b2727e938af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{11\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294244}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d621446-a4c1-5070-b626-77ed872c6c20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294241} :answer-field/id #uuid "918661ac-38f4-5659-b7bd-83f2c2390cde" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294239}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4653a01e-6fa4-5345-8443-e064ea5e4a3d" :question/math-academy-id "q-120789" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14035241/assets/8197d1111bbd4c3b8908bc12cbc58561ffd23fe06d9f9fa99f9ab815ed78733f.png)\n\nThe graph shows a portion of the curve $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and $y=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ Which of the following generates the $x$-values of all of their points of intersection? Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "At the intersection points, the $y$-values are the same for both graphs. This gives the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ \n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $2$nd quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14035241/assets/ba34bf96662682ec433c3ade0bf3f118638212101a0729fe95bbae05aee8bf1e.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$  lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution for the given equation is \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258090 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b12a7b7-1df3-5203-934e-8820604de3de" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Expressions in Radians Using the Sum and Difference Formulas"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c800bf1-db0e-5a4f-b1f7-79de46c24ed9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294266} {:answer/id #uuid "7bdc1a32-3ddc-5b62-b443-798708784e12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294267} {:answer/id #uuid "8fbfce8f-1539-5097-be10-dbae597cac5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294268} {:answer/id #uuid "2ee8162a-22a0-5c9e-b9db-f9b656661d56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294269} {:answer/id #uuid "976eda39-25fa-5c0c-ada1-63cab5aa0334" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294270}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8fbfce8f-1539-5097-be10-dbae597cac5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294268} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9a0acda-284b-5a31-8855-d2a8a1ff7df1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294265}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4878f13e-2679-5b9e-8cae-9094f9bb2d55" :question/math-academy-id "q-141281" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+\\frac{2\\pi }{3})=$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for sine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{2\\pi }{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247199 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e274fe1-7242-5a6c-8351-eeff9877d2bf" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Quotient of Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8353b5dc-7cd7-5dd4-8896-24138ab0ad3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247201} {:answer/id #uuid "b8ce6c33-30ba-5cb1-83a3-61b4b3b415ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247202} {:answer/id #uuid "4c8c732c-3a67-5773-95c5-05e65b555e6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247203} {:answer/id #uuid "8f17eeee-6e1a-53a8-937d-9232a4cc2c10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247204} {:answer/id #uuid "6cce4235-8958-5494-8a97-872bec019cb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247205}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6cce4235-8958-5494-8a97-872bec019cb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247205} :answer-field/id #uuid "10cf0431-cc21-5deb-8a40-4019c358ec69" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247200}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "835d558d-3fba-579b-91a8-f50eb4c92637" :question/math-academy-id "q-15664" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934289/assets/e4b9bd92b63d85b0336974285b6aa7a8a7beb8348cf368f65d805f7b4b70ffa2.png)\n\nGiven the plots of $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ above, find ${h}^{′}(1)$ where $h(x)=\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.$" :question/worked-solution "The quotient rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)\\cdot g(x)-f(x)\\cdot {g}^{′}(x)}{(g(x))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(1)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(1)\\cdot g(1)-f(1)\\cdot {g}^{′}(1)}{(g(1))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph above, we obtain that $f(1)=3$ and $g(1)=2.$\n\nAdditionally:\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (0,2)$ equals $1.$ So, ${f}^{′}(1)=1.$\n\n- The slope of $g(x)$ on $x\\in (0,3)$ equals $-1.$ So, ${g}^{′}(1)=-1.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(1)\\cdot g(1)-f(1)\\cdot {g}^{′}(1)}{(g(1))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 2-3\\cdot (-1)}{{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279075 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "89362140-1a17-56fd-a9ab-4da336112bee" :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying a Matrix by a Column or Row of the Identity Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d130fa2-0493-59f0-af50-5160da776d28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279077} {:answer/id #uuid "51168f06-2e01-5b0b-8693-e06b3de9dde2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5} \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279078} {:answer/id #uuid "bbd472d0-73ef-50e9-855a-0399cfed9979" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5} & 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279079} {:answer/id #uuid "4460027c-0c38-5e6a-8d86-3dc9b9c03c6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2\\sqrt{2} & -3.2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279080} {:answer/id #uuid "3d480acf-3659-5c9e-a0aa-59046b467d2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279081}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4460027c-0c38-5e6a-8d86-3dc9b9c03c6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2\\sqrt{2} & -3.2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279080} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5abea32-c0b7-548b-8c88-75fbbdda6178" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279076}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c7963c06-fa30-5a29-ac72-ec8ff733ff33" :question/math-academy-id "q-41944" :question/problem "Find the product $AB$, if\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]$\nand \n$B=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5} & 4 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\sqrt{2} & -3.2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $A$ is the $2$nd *row* of the identity matrix ${I}_{3}.$\n\nSince we're multiplying the $3\\times 3$ matrix $B$ by the $2$nd *row* of the identity matrix ${I}_{3}$ on the *left*, the result must be the $2$nd *row* of $B.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nAB=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2\\sqrt{2} & -3.2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nLet's check the long way:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5} & 4 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\sqrt{2} & -3.2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2\\sqrt{2} & -3.2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260488 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a0cc5ea-7f70-5c5f-841e-01a6813da8c6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Operations on Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ebf2e9d7-31ca-5d03-bbcb-392994bc39ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260490} {:answer/id #uuid "063a9d47-c2f3-5ee8-888d-19388957813d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260491} {:answer/id #uuid "354dfa5c-933f-56e0-b784-e3778f7b0c95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260492} {:answer/id #uuid "5f4242d4-2b03-5e93-8f4c-e52fbd49c565" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260493} {:answer/id #uuid "ff96f702-8d09-5553-8e60-afeac0d3cb05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260494} {:answer/id #uuid "efb3abad-5ae7-5dde-8f68-1f4ed8247a31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260495} {:answer/id #uuid "7342fdd3-89f9-5282-9690-bc9b44b19fbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260496} {:answer/id #uuid "7f067afb-9173-5325-99a3-8593dd6e211c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260497}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff96f702-8d09-5553-8e60-afeac0d3cb05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260494} :answer-field/id #uuid "71f95d56-7ec8-583a-a3ec-79cbc7ddc998" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260489}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4c510ba2-7634-5ceb-8cb3-a5b35044f36d" :question/math-academy-id "q-47447" :question/problem "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $3$ and $7,$ respectively, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. - $P+Q$ is always a polynomial of degree $10$\nII. - $P-Q$ is always a polynomial of degree $4$\nIII. - $P\\cdot Q$ is always a polynomial of degree $10$" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, suppose $P(x)={x}^{3}$ and $Q(x)={x}^{7}.$ Then $P+Q={x}^{3}+{x}^{7},$ which is not a polynomial of degree $10.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, suppose $P(x)={x}^{3}$ and $Q(x)={x}^{7}.$ Then $P-Q={x}^{3}-{x}^{7},$ which is not a polynomial of degree $4.$\n\n- Statement III is true. The highest-degree terms in $P$ and $Q$ must contain ${x}^{3}$ and ${x}^{7},$ respectively. Therefore, the highest-degree term in $P\\cdot Q$ must contain ${x}^{3}\\cdot {x}^{7}={x}^{3+7}={x}^{10},$ meaning that $P\\cdot Q$ has degree $10.$\n\nIn conclusion, only statement III is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260869 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "831f9aa0-099e-5e0c-8a9d-c4828b629f64" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Expressions in Radians Using the Sum and Difference Formulas"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f10507d-8411-501b-bf2a-099f1599108a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294349} {:answer/id #uuid "6b40c74f-5636-54fa-8e48-1cc8dc48453b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294350} {:answer/id #uuid "a8890cb1-796d-5e73-9f8e-477954728915" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294351} {:answer/id #uuid "f886e3e2-bc69-5e9f-8cd6-9f9f6b4e1c40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294352} {:answer/id #uuid "ef9f71ba-6aae-578e-ae86-a1f2d811e951" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294353}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b40c74f-5636-54fa-8e48-1cc8dc48453b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294350} :answer-field/id #uuid "0028443c-1e65-58fd-b318-c517c386e7d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294348}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8bb03c0c-2bf3-5dda-8102-58698d5cc17e" :question/math-academy-id "q-65502" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+\\frac{2\\pi }{3})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for cosine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261365 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ecb9df79-5227-5fc6-9a20-818fee34e971" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Right Riemann Sum With Irregular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "243bae6a-7f90-509c-a611-d0cd92e56106" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "55" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273288} {:answer/id #uuid "5f1350aa-51a5-5918-9989-a72de659b6ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(55)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273289} {:answer/id #uuid "e3685375-90e3-5409-b4a8-619b07aa258e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(55)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273290} {:answer/id #uuid "bd9a05e4-f6ec-5ed5-82f3-6f76662411d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(55)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273291} {:answer/id #uuid "5d29a48d-bc3f-54a0-a202-80512e7411b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(55)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273292}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "243bae6a-7f90-509c-a611-d0cd92e56106" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "55" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273288} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc98e4b3-0f9d-51fe-abc9-ac2aaa742645" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274941}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "71771640-36aa-5365-9496-11f66f198e7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-72064" :question/problem "The following table shows the number of customers per minute, $C(t),$ that arrive at a bank, measured over a period of $15$ minutes.\n\n| $t\\,\\text{(minutes)}$ | $0$ | $3$ | $7$ | $12$ | $15$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $C(t)\\,\\text{(customers/minute)}$ | $3$ | $4$ | $3$ | $5$ | $2$ |\n\nUse the right Riemann sum over $[0,15]$ with four subintervals to approximate the total number of customers served." :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ denote the area under the curve $y=C(t)$ over $[0,15].$ In this case, we have $n=4$ rectangles. Using the right Riemann sum, the area is approximated as \n\n$$\nA\\approx C(3)Δ{t}_{1}+C(7)Δ{t}_{2}+C(12)Δ{t}_{3}+C(15)Δ{t}_{4},\n$$\n\nwhere each $Δ{t}_{i}$ is calculated by considering the step between a particular $t$-value and the previous one, i.e.,\n\n$$\nΔ{t}_{i}={t}_{i}-{t}_{i-1}.\n$$\n\nWe set up a value table that includes the $Δt$'s.\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $3$ | $7$ | $12$ | $15$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $Δt$ | $-$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $3$ |\n| $C(t)$ | $-$ | $4$ | $3$ | $5$ | $2$ |\n\nWe plug in the numbers to approximate the area under the curve and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & \\approx 4\\cdot 3+3\\cdot 4+5\\cdot 5+2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =12+12+25+6 \\\\  & =55.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in the given context, the area under the curve represents the total number of customers served over a period of $15$ minutes. So, the total number of customers is approximately\n$55.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261546 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "00d82333-e4e0-5527-a084-0af8affe5bee" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Left Riemann Sum With Irregular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6876d967-13f8-5ac6-aa24-eac1c9701487" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273476} {:answer/id #uuid "2c8c42d7-89f3-5e87-9191-8b057539f2e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3110" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273477} {:answer/id #uuid "cd9bb0e9-442b-5e14-8e5d-a80e29e93455" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1720" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273478} {:answer/id #uuid "9bbeb22e-c5b5-5eed-9760-0ab182a73efd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1070" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273479} {:answer/id #uuid "6c132005-423a-5b49-b552-b69bcd914e9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2140" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273480}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6876d967-13f8-5ac6-aa24-eac1c9701487" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273476} :answer-field/id #uuid "29eed7a1-3c5f-594c-8775-e226be9c8e29" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274979}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "41763944-ffc1-5cbe-ae34-8d6954078ad7" :question/math-academy-id "q-74505" :question/problem "The following table shows some values of a continuous function $f(x)$ over the interval $[2,12].$\n\n| $x$ | $2$ | $4$ | $8$ | $10$ | $12$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $5$ | $3$ | $2$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n\nUse the left Riemann sum, with the four subintervals indicated by the data, to approximate the area under the curve $y=f(x)$ over the interval $[2,12].$" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have $n=4$ rectangles. Using the left Riemann sum, the area is approximated as\n\n$$\nA\\approx f(2)Δ{x}_{1}+f(4)Δ{x}_{2}+f(8)Δ{x}_{3}+f(10)Δ{x}_{4},\n$$\n\nwhere each $Δ{x}_{i}$ is calculated by considering the step between a particular $x$-value and the next one, i.e.,\n\n$$\nΔ{x}_{i}={x}_{i+1}-{x}_{i}.\n$$\n\nWe set up a value table that includes the $Δx$'s.\n\n| $x$ | $2$ | $4$ | $8$ | $10$ | $12$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $Δx$ | $2$ | $4$ | $2$ | $2$ | $-$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $5$ | $3$ | $2$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, we plug in the numbers to approximate the area under the curve and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & \\approx 5\\cdot 2+3\\cdot 4+2\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =10+12+4+2 \\\\  & =28.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261599 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c698912-1f35-5110-8d5d-d490cce2e9a7" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations Containing Quadratic Denominators"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b6e52b7c-0f41-5e6a-b88c-323acbfef6e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273551} {:answer/id #uuid "7c987a70-a554-57f2-a7a5-596ecc2715bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273552} {:answer/id #uuid "708a7f93-7c0a-5350-b4aa-d99797a71d1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273553} {:answer/id #uuid "9157de8b-eaeb-5151-842c-b5d458782cac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273554} {:answer/id #uuid "d0637c39-5487-5972-9e2e-acc5a04d2948" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273555}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6e52b7c-0f41-5e6a-b88c-323acbfef6e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273551} :answer-field/id #uuid "5acb0b7e-4183-537a-a773-7b0e0cce9857" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274994}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c7212979-12af-55e0-b574-fe7390124c7f" :question/math-academy-id "q-75961" :question/problem "Which value of $y$ satisfies the equation $\\frac{y+1}{{y}^{2}}=\\frac{3}{2y}+\\frac{2y-1}{{y}^{2}}?$" :question/worked-solution "We note that $y=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators ${y}^{2}$ and $2y.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}({y}^{2},2y)=2{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $2{y}^{2},$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y+1}{{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{3}{2y}+\\frac{2y-1}{{y}^{2}} \\\\ 2{y}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{y+1}{{y}^{2}}) & =2{y}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2y}+\\frac{2y-1}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\ 2{y}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{y+1}{{y}^{2}}) & =2{y}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2y})+2{y}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{2y-1}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\ 2\\cdot {y}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{y+1}{{y}^{2}}) & =y\\cdot 2y\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2y})+2\\cdot {y}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{2y-1}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\ 2\\cdot \\cancel{{y}^{2}}\\cdot (\\frac{y+1}{\\cancel{{y}^{2}}}) & =y\\cdot \\cancel{2y}\\cdot (\\frac{3}{\\cancel{2y}})+2\\cdot \\cancel{{y}^{2}}\\cdot (\\frac{2y-1}{\\cancel{{y}^{2}}}) \\\\ 2(y+1) & =3y+2(2y-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $y,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(y+1) & =3y+2(2y-1) \\\\ 2y+2 & =7y-2 \\\\ 7y-2y & =2+2 \\\\ 5y & =4 \\\\ y & =\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $y=0$ as a solution. But since $y=\\frac{4}{5}\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $y=\\frac{4}{5}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294379 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "83fad21d-6b36-5198-a13f-362d34342475" :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying Complex Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9921209-dae9-5088-9de7-05c7fd47e501" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-7-2\\text{i} \\\\ 1-2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294381} {:answer/id #uuid "3754d50a-db5d-5ff0-b7b6-372317e63a2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1+2\\text{i} \\\\ -7+2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294382} {:answer/id #uuid "c9681e1b-016c-5383-b6d6-a29ae4e874ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7-2\\text{i} \\\\ -1-2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294383} {:answer/id #uuid "bd707435-20e3-5699-af51-97510db3a9ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1-2\\text{i} \\\\ 7-2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294384} {:answer/id #uuid "4fb85375-392a-5d81-a298-78fb82c7fe13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1+2\\text{i} \\\\ 7+2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294385}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd707435-20e3-5699-af51-97510db3a9ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1-2\\text{i} \\\\ 7-2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294384} :answer-field/id #uuid "79d7745d-f9d6-5352-a971-0ad567d7937d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294380}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "93cd3e49-db0b-5ee5-918c-8c97827d57f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-76209" :question/problem "If $\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} & 2-\\text{i} \\\\ 1-2\\text{i} & 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}1+2\\text{i} \\\\ 1-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]\\,$, then $\\,Ax=$" :question/worked-solution "Multiplying the matrix by the vector using the usual method, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax & =[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} & 2-\\text{i} \\\\ 1-2\\text{i} & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1+2\\text{i} \\\\ 1-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i}(1+2\\text{i})+(2-\\text{i})(1-\\text{i}) \\\\ (1-2\\text{i})(1+2\\text{i})+2(1-\\text{i})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(\\text{i}-2)+(1-3\\text{i}) \\\\ 5+(2-2\\text{i})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1-2\\text{i} \\\\ 7-2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097288 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2487fa68-ae0e-5137-baea-4f3fc09c2953" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Quadratic Sequence Is Monotonic"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d5347864-abba-582b-9d10-58a98e87ea0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186096086} {:answer/id #uuid "e87df1aa-b3ed-5b0c-8c29-0d919ce7cab8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186096087} {:answer/id #uuid "38be679b-a71e-5304-b482-e88bc16ace48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186096088} {:answer/id #uuid "51712480-f571-5a03-80c0-0c2742b595e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186096089} {:answer/id #uuid "67531f4e-2463-59a8-9aab-fbebc2829491" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186096090}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67531f4e-2463-59a8-9aab-fbebc2829491" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186096090} :answer-field/id #uuid "f419a350-3170-5600-a1b9-5f2d7f562216" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186096085}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a3853e50-9001-5dfb-9817-609b2adc1080" :question/math-academy-id "q-81083" :question/problem "Given that $a_{n} = n^{2} - 5$ for $n \\ge 1$, which of the following statements are true?\n\